WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
What are AI guardrails?

2
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.440
Guardrails are controls designed to reduce unsafe, unwanted,

3
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.880
or invalid AI behavior.

4
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.160
They do not promise perfection.

5
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.720
They add checks and limits around a model

6
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.320
so the whole system can behave more safely.

7
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.480
Guardrails can appear at several points in one workflow.

8
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.640
A user sends input.

9
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.160
An input guardrail checks it.

10
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.200
The model produces a response or proposes an action.

11
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.000
Tool and permission controls decide what the system is allowed to do.

12
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.960
Output validation checks the result before it reaches the user or another system.

13
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.680
A human can approve sensitive actions when required.

14
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.440
There are five useful categories.

15
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.720
Input controls check or filter incoming content,

16
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.000
such as blocking a known prompt injection pattern.

17
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.560
Output controls validate generated results,

18
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.240
such as rejecting an answer that does not match a required format.

19
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.600
Tool controls limit available actions,

20
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.960
such as allowing search but not deletion.

21
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.320
Permission controls restrict access to systems and data,

22
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.200
such as denying payment release.

23
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.640
Human approval adds explicit review for a sensitive action,

24
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.000
such as a large refund.

25
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.280
Consider an AI refund agent.

26
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.560
A user says refund $10,000 immediately.

27
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.440
The model may propose the refund,

28
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.360
but the system does not have to execute it.

29
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.400
First, an amount check can identify that the request is high value.

30
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.240
Next, a permission check can ask whether this agent is allowed to release money.

31
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.120
Then a human can review the action.

32
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.120
The refund executes only if the controls allow it.

33
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.680
Keep four distinctions clear.

34
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.320
A guardrail reduces risk.

35
00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.800
A guarantee would mean that failure cannot happen.

36
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.800
Guardrails improve safety,

37
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.120
but they do not make an AI system infallible.

38
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.560
Permission is narrower.

39
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.720
A guardrail is the broader control concept.

40
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.640
Permission is a specific rule about what an action or resource allows.

41
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:23.480
For example, a guardrail may say that a high value payment needs a control.

42
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.920
The permission rule may say that this agent cannot release the payment.

43
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.880
Validation is also narrower.

44
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.080
Validation checks whether something meets defined rules.

45
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.120
A guardrail is broader and may include validation,

46
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.680
permissions, filters, or approval gates.

47
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.600
Human approval is a person explicitly reviewing or authorizing an action.

48
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.840
A guardrail can be automated or human controlled,

49
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.120
so human approval can be one type of guardrail.

50
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.160
Real systems use defense and depth.

51
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:03.960
They may combine an input check, a system instruction, tool restriction,

52
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.520
permission enforcement, output validation, and human approval.

53
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.000
No single control handles every risk.

54
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.680
Layers make it harder for one mistake to become an unsafe outcome.

55
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:18.920
Remember this.

56
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:25.280
Guardrails reduce AI system risk by constraining inputs, outputs, actions, and access.

